you might have treated your hair
this is something I am very familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.580
with I think we all know that chemical&nbsp;
treatments like perms dyes straightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.660
and bleaching are really damaging but&nbsp;
it is a bit more complicated than that&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.860
you can't just use a more concentrated&nbsp;
conditioner these treatments will actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.840
change the chemical structure of your hair so&nbsp;
it won't interact with ingredients the same way&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.020
the main thing we care about&nbsp;
here is oxidative damage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.660
the main place this happens is called the F layer

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.740
you might have seen the structure of hair&nbsp;
before if we zoom into the surface of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.820
outside layer called the cuticle there&nbsp;
is this thin layer of oily substances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:59.580
attached this is called 18-methyleicosanoic acid
it's like your hair's natural hair conditioner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.360
that is permanently bonded to your hair&nbsp;
when you treat your hair with some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.680
of oxidizing chemical treatment a lot of&nbsp;
this will break off and you're left with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.000
these groups which are called sulfonates
these are not oily they're on the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.380
end of the chemistry spectrum where water is&nbsp;
and like water they don't really stick to oil&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.360
and this is a problem because a lot of hair&nbsp;
conditioning ingredients are oily they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.640
stick to the oily F layer on less damaged hair&nbsp;
but they will slide right off super damaged hair&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.020
so hair conditioners that are designed for&nbsp;
less damaged hair are just not going to work&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:36.240
it doesn't matter how much you use plus your hair&nbsp;
is more damaged so it's getting less conditioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.200
while also needing more conditioning
this is one big reason why I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.280
a lot of people have a sort of crisis&nbsp;
after they radically change their hair&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.160
I definitely did
it felt like I just ruined my hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.780
and there was no going back but it turned out I&nbsp;
just needed a different hair conditioner with more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.220
positively charged conditioning ingredients
these new sulfonate groups on the hair are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.340
negatively charged most of the&nbsp;
time and opposite charges attract&nbsp;

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.260
so these positive conditioning ingredients will&nbsp;
stick more strongly to the more damaged parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.280
your hair which is exactly what you want
these ingredients are also self-limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.720
which means that once you have enough attached to&nbsp;
your hair it will get a slightly positive charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.980
so these positively charged ingredients&nbsp;
will have a much harder time sticking on&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.420
so that means your hair is less likely to get&nbsp;
weighed down if you're using more conditioner&nbsp;

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.720
here are some examples of uncharged&nbsp;
and charged conditioning ingredients&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.900
now all hair conditioners will&nbsp;
have a mix of both categories&nbsp;

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:33.000
but conditioners for damaged hair will usually&nbsp;
